    "narrative": "For this May 11 slate, the **Zurich Zebras** offense is anchored by **Junior Caminero** (67.23) and **Yordan Alvarez** (64.21), who represent our highest-ceiling plays against Gausman and Kirby, respectively. **Junior Caminero** benefits significantly from the high-leverage #2 spot in the order combined with a favorable park factor, while **Yordan Alvarez** remains a locked-in start despite facing a high-caliber arm; his elite 0.429 xwOBA provides the necessary insulation against the SP skill penalty. The most aggressive upside play is **Casey Schmitt** (63.20), whose score is inflated by a dream matchup against Sasaki (5.30 xERA). **Casey Schmitt**\u2019s status as a #3 hitter, paired with a significant dynamic platoon advantage, makes him an essential piece of today\u2019s production puzzle.\n\nWhile the primary lineup is settled, the bench remains impacted by scheduling inefficiencies. **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, **Trea Turner**, and **Kyle Schwarber** are all unavailable due to off-days, and **Christian Yelich** remains sidelined on the IL, leaving no immediate threats to the current starters. **Kazuma Okamoto** (49.61) and **Luke Raley** (52.38) are positioned as marginal plays; both have their raw projections tempered by difficult opposing SPs (Rasmussen and Lambert, respectively). **Hyeseong Kim** (40.69) sits at the very bottom of our viable threshold, and owners should be prepared for a diminished return given the elite 2.60 xERA of McDonald, which effectively nukes the efficiency of his #9 spot in the lineup.\n\nIn terms of risk management, we are operating without the luxury of our elite speed assets like **Trea Turner** or **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, which puts extra pressure on **Hyeseong Kim** to be opportunistic on the basepaths today. We continue to monitor the status of our depth pieces including **Caleb Durbin**, **Colt Keith**, **Zac Veen**, **Lars Nootbaar**, **Chase Meidroth**, **Gary Sanchez**, **Josh Smith**, **Dillon Dingler**, **Carson Benge**, **Pedro Ramirez**, and **Luis Pena** for potential future rotations as the schedule tightens. For today, the strategy is clear: trust the top-order hitters and look for **Casey Schmitt** to exploit the weak opposing starter to carry the offense.",
